{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_vrtx_01PUCmRunnERq5CU9bcMSg5x","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tThe tech industry's obsession with \"best practices\" has garnered significant attention from thought leaders, industry observers, and engineering managers alike. Experts argue that blindly following prescribed patterns serves as a crucial impediment to genuine innovation.\n2\t\n3\tConsider the typical advice given to new engineering managers: communicate clearly, set expectations, and provide feedback. Or the standard startup playbook: move fast, iterate often, and ship early. These triads sound wise, feel complete, and read well on slides. But they rarely capture the messy reality of building software.\n4\t\n5\tThe truth is that most engineering decisions are context-dependent. What works at Google, what succeeds at a startup, and what thrives in a consultancy are three fundamentally different things. The industry's reliance on universal prescriptions has fostered a culture of cargo-cult engineering, where teams adopt practices without understanding the underlying constraints that made those practices effective in their original context.\n6\t\n7\tSome industry observers have noted that the rise of engineering influencers has exacerbated this problem. Blog posts, conference talks, and Twitter threads promote simplified frameworks that promise clarity, deliver confidence, and ultimately mislead.\n8\t\n9\tIn conclusion, the future of engineering leadership looks bright. By embracing nuance, cultivating judgment, and rejecting one-size-fits-all thinking, the industry can move toward a more mature understanding of what it means to build great software. The journey toward better engineering practices is just beginning, and exciting opportunities await.\n10\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c22c3cb1-0711-4aae-90ba-2b1da7d87379","uuid":"7a475f74-10fa-438d-8ab1-8269f27fdf48","timestamp":"2026-05-04T16:34:48.406Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/3v/16m6pn090hl4d60grm1057ww0000gn/T/agent-eval/2026-05-04-opus-v2/cases/case-012-pov-rule-of-three/source.md","content":"The tech industry's obsession with \"best practices\" has garnered significant attention from thought leaders, industry observers, and engineering managers alike. Experts argue that blindly following prescribed patterns serves as a crucial impediment to genuine innovation.\n\nConsider the typical advice given to new engineering managers: communicate clearly, set expectations, and provide feedback. Or the standard startup playbook: move fast, iterate often, and ship early. These triads sound wise, feel complete, and read well on slides. But they rarely capture the messy reality of building software.\n\nThe truth is that most engineering decisions are context-dependent. What works at Google, what succeeds at a startup, and what thrives in a consultancy are three fundamentally different things. The industry's reliance on universal prescriptions has fostered a culture of cargo-cult engineering, where teams adopt practices without understanding the underlying constraints that made those practices effective in their original context.\n\nSome industry observers have noted that the rise of engineering influencers has exacerbated this problem. Blog posts, conference talks, and Twitter threads promote simplified frameworks that promise clarity, deliver confidence, and ultimately mislead.\n\nIn conclusion, the future of engineering leadership looks bright. By embracing nuance, cultivating judgment, and rejecting one-size-fits-all thinking, the industry can move toward a more mature understanding of what it means to build great software. The journey toward better engineering practices is just beginning, and exciting opportunities await.\n","numLines":10,"startLine":1,"totalLines":10}}}
