p looks bright. By embracing nuance, cultivating judgment, and rejecting one-size-fits-all thinking, the industry can move toward a more mature understanding of what it means to build great software. The journey toward better engineering practices is just beginning, and exciting opportunities await.","newString":"The tech industry loves \"best practices.\" It loves them so much it stops asking whether they actually work.\n\nConsider the typical advice given to new engineering managers: communicate clearly, set expectations, provide feedback. Or the startup playbook: move fast, iterate, ship early. These sound wise on slides. They rarely capture the messy reality of building software, because they are not supposed to. They are bumper stickers.\n\nMost engineering decisions are context-dependent. What works at Google does not work at a 12-person startup, and neither translates to a consultancy billing by the hour. But teams keep adopting practices wholesale, without understanding the constraints that made those practices effective somewhere else. That is cargo-cult engineering.\n\nEngineering influencers have made this worse. Simplified frameworks get packaged into blog posts and conference talks, where they sound authoritative and land poorly. A thread that gets 10k likes is not evidence that the advice is good. It is evidence that the advice is tweetable.\n\nI do not have a tidy answer here. Engineering judgment is hard to teach because it depends on things you can only learn by getting them wrong. But I am fairly sure that copying someone else's \"three pillars of engineering excellence\" is not it.","originalFile":"The tech industry's obsession with \"best practices\" has garnered significant attention from thought leaders, industry observers, and engineering managers alike. Experts argue that blindly following prescribed patterns serves as a crucial impediment to genuine innovation.\n\nConsider the typical advice given to new engineering managers: communicate clearly, set expectations, and provide feedback. Or the standard startup playbook: move fast, iterate often, and ship early. These triads sound wise, feel complete, and read well on slides. But they rarely capture the messy reality of building software.\n\nThe truth is that most engineering decisions are context-dependent. What works at Google, what succeeds at a startup, and what thrives in a consultancy are three fundamentally different things. The industry's reliance on universal prescriptions has fostered a culture of cargo-cult engineering, where teams adopt practices without understanding the underlying constraints that made those practices effective in their original context.\n\nSome industry observers have noted that the rise of engineering influencers has exacerbated this problem. Blog posts, conference talks, and Twitter threads promote simplified frameworks that promise clarity, deliver confidence, and ultimately mislead.\n\nIn conclusion, the future of engineering leadership looks bright. By embracing nuance, cultivating judgment, and rejecting one-size-fits-all thinking, the industry can move toward a more mature understanding of what it means to build great software. The journey toward better engineering practices is just beginning, and exciting opportunities await.\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":1,"oldLines":9,"newStart":1,"newLines":9,"lines":["-The tech industry's obsession with \"best practices\" has garnered significant attention from thought leaders, industry observers, and engineering managers alike. Experts argue that blindly following prescribed patterns serves as a crucial impediment to genuine innovation.","+The tech industry loves \"best practices.\" It loves them so much it stops asking whether they actually work."," ","-Consider the typical advice given to new engineering managers: communicate clearly, set expectations, and provide feedback. Or the standard startup playbook: move fast, iterate often, and ship early. These triads sound wise, feel complete, and read well on slides. But they rarely capture the messy reality of building software.","+Consider the typical advice given to new engineering managers: communicate clearly, set expectations, provide feedback. Or the startup playbook: move fast, iterate, ship early. These sound wise on slides. They rarely capture the messy reality of building software, because they are not supposed to. They are bumper stickers."," ","-The truth is that most engineering decisions are context-dependent. What works at Google, what succeeds at a startup, and what thrives in a consultancy are three fundamentally different things. The industry's reliance on universal prescriptions has fostered a culture of cargo-cult engineering, where teams adopt practices without understanding the underlying constraints that made those practices effective in their original context.","+Most engineering decisions are context-dependent. What works at Google does not work at a 12-person startup, and neither translates to a consultancy billing by the hour. But teams keep adopting practices wholesale, without understanding the constraints that made those practices effective somewhere else. That is cargo-cult engineering."," ","-Some industry observers have noted that the rise of engineering influencers has exacerbated this problem. Blog posts, conference talks, and Twitter threads promote simplified frameworks that promise clarity, deliver confidence, and ultimately mislead.","+Engineering influencers have made this worse. Simplified frameworks get packaged into blog posts and conference talks, where they sound authoritative and land poorly. A thread that gets 10k likes is not evidence that the advice is good. It is evidence that the advice is tweetable."," ","-In conclusion, the future of engineering leadership looks bright. By embracing nuance, cultivating judgment, and rejecting one-size-fits-all thinking, the industry can move toward a more mature understanding of what it means to build great software. The journey toward better engineering practices is just beginning, and exciting opportunities await.","+I do not have a tidy answer here. Engineering judgment is hard to teach because it depends on things you can only learn by getting them wrong. But I am fairly sure that copying someone else's \"three pillars of engineering excellence\" is not it."]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false}}
